<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wartburg Festival</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Wartburgfest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> Festival was a gathering of about 500 German students at the Wartburg castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> on 18 October 1817, marking the 300th anniversary of Luther's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the fourth anniversary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a>. Students called for national unity and burned books they considered reactionary. Together with later events, it alarmed the German governments and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> of 1819.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wartburg-festival.jpg" alt="Wartburg Festival" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wartburg_demonstration_1817.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Wars of Liberation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, many people were bitter that hopes of national unity had been dashed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815, while democratic reform stalled and governments curbed the press and the right of association. On 12 June 1815 students at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> founded the Urburschenschaft to promote German unity. Many had fought as volunteers against Napoleon, some in the Lützow Free Corps, whose black, red and gold colours were later adopted for the Flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>The Burschenschaft fraternities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Halle chose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> had found refuge there after Emperor Charles V declared him an outlaw in 1521, and had translated the Bible there, which made the castle a symbol of German nationalism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Students came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlangen">Erlangen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, Rostock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> and other universities, joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> professors such as Lorenz Oken, Heinrich Luden and Jakob Friedrich Fries. In the knights' hall speakers presented Luther as a fighter for freedom and spoke of the way to national unity. The singing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/now-thank-we-all-our-god">Now Thank We All Our God</a> and a closing blessing gave the gathering the air of a Protestant service, and at the festive meal toasts were raised to the fallen of the Liberation Wars, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Scharnhorst</a>, Schill and Körner.</p><p>After the official programme, followers of Friedrich Ludwig Jahn staged a book burning in imitation of Luther's burning of the papal bull in 1520. Mock books stood for works such as Karl Ludwig von Haller's Restoration of Political Science, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-von-kotzebue">August von Kotzebue</a>'s History of the German Realm, Saul Ascher's Germanomanie and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, and a corporal's cane was burned as a symbol of foreign rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Metternich's government had already kept Austrian universities from taking part, and the festival was used to justify further measures against liberal forces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambach-festival">Hambacher Fest</a> of 1832 was held in a similar manner, and a second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> festival took place during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a>. In 1933 the students' book burning was invoked to justify the Nazi book burnings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wartburg Festival” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its repressive policies made it a symbol of reaction to liberals and nationalists, but within its framework constitutional government took root in the southern states, and the national movement grew through gymnastics clubs, choral societies, student fraternities and the <b>Wartburg Festival</b> of 1817.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Young men dressed like Werther and later imitated Byron; volunteers from many countries went to fight for Greek independence; German students gathered at the <b>Wartburg Festival</b> of 1817 to call for national unity.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany it was also sung as a patriotic song, for example at the <b>Wartburg Festival</b> of 1817, since many Protestants saw the Reformation as a national as well as a religious movement.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li></ul></section>
